var e=`# CHAPTER 1 — Where I Am Today\r
\r
Fights of reality. Let's go.\r
\r
This is kind of hard to talk about.\r
\r
---\r
\r
This project has been a dream since I was 17. Watching it come out is really exciting. Beautiful to see, for real.\r
\r
Even with my situation being complicated. Really complicated.\r
\r
And I decided to tell it. Not so anyone feels sorry for me. I'm living this and I don't feel sorry for myself. I'm living this and I keep working every single day.\r
\r
So keep your pity. That's not what this is about.\r
\r
---\r
\r
Let me explain how I got here.\r
\r
The games industry collapsed. Mass layoffs at Epic Games. Then mass layoffs at Xbox itself, at Microsoft.\r
\r
I felt it a little before. I felt the market was heading somewhere that didn't look stable at all. And the video game market, on its own, was never stable. It runs on projects. There's a huge difference between keeping a product alive long term and keeping a product alive until one specific launch. It ships, the project ends, the job ends.\r
\r
So I started turning things around. I moved into traditional software development.\r
\r
Today I'm at [Panorama.id](https://panoramaid.com.br). A really cool company that gave me my career back. This time as a traditional developer, outside of games. I don't know if I'll still be there by launch. But today I'm there.\r
\r
And I love games. I love them so much this project has games in it. A lot of my experience is in it. After working on God of War: Sons of Sparta and Five Nights at Freddy's: Into the Pit, it was obvious to me that I'd go out and make the games I always dreamed of making.\r
\r
Except reality is harder than it looks.\r
\r
---\r
\r
Reality isn't as pretty as the lives of Kim, Jack and Nina.\r
\r
And that's why I don't make their lives pretty. It would be forcing it.\r
\r
I'm not knocking anyone who writes pretty, lovely, wonderful, happy stories. I admire it. I like reading stories like that. But I can't make that. Not that way. I can't spit that out from inside me.\r
\r
---\r
\r
Starting over has a price.\r
\r
My salary dropped a lot. And my cost of living today is higher than the salary I get.\r
\r
So, to make up for it, every day at 6 p.m. I head out on my bike.\r
\r
The Warrior. That's her name.\r
\r
She works with me. She helps me a lot.\r
\r
So, my people, this is life. I went back to the streets. And there's nothing wrong with that. I don't see anything bad in it. Actually, sometimes I really enjoy it. Not every day, obviously. But sometimes.\r
\r
---\r
\r
I started out on the streets. At 13.\r
\r
And I ended up back on the streets at 40.\r
\r
That's how the world works.\r
\r
I remember being a little kid, sixth, seventh grade, talking to a friend of mine named Lester. And he said:\r
\r
"Man, life is a slot car track, bro. You just go around."\r
\r
It's really funny. Some twenty-five years later, rounding it off, life going around again. Back on the streets.\r
\r
---\r
\r
But this isn't supposed to be a sob story. That's really important.\r
\r
I'm not counting on this product being a success. To be really honest, it could even hurt. Demand could show up that I can't handle, can't treat the way it deserves, simply because I don't have the resources.\r
\r
I've been coding this whole project since June. The story was built when I was 17. Three years ago I put out a book I didn't like. I pulled it. I said: I'm doing it again, and this time I'm doing it differently.\r
\r
All of this was supposed to be a simple blog. I looked at where I could publish it as a web novel and thought: I'm not getting any visibility there. And if there's no visibility, at least it stays under my control.\r
\r
But here's the big truth: what 17-year-old Isaias dreamed up became 40-year-old Isaias's job to deliver.\r
\r
---\r
\r
I'm not strong. I don't have superpowers. I don't do magic.\r
\r
I go through hard times like everybody. And today I'm going through some really extreme ones. Really extreme.\r
\r
I always thought the peak of the hardship in my life was a crowd fight I was in, around 19, 20. I'd been in other crowd fights, but always with a group. That was the first time I went into one alone.\r
\r
And I thought: this is the peak of my survival. If I survived this, if I held up through this, I'll survive anything.\r
\r
One day I'll tell you that one.\r
\r
---\r
\r
My stories won't be in order. It'll be whatever I feel like telling. Whatever I think is wise to tell. Because there's a lot of complicated stuff. Really complicated.\r
\r
And I'll be extremely honest, like I always am. I won't fictionalize. I won't lie, because I don't lie. I'll tell you the facts.\r
\r
Some of those facts you'll look at and say: no, that's not possible. And that's normal. It just didn't happen to you. That's fine.\r
\r
Jumping off a bridge. Taking on a big religious ministry. And, who knows, even the possibility of ending up paying a little visit to the system.\r
\r
It's part of it. We make choices. I don't regret the choices I made, and you have to live with the consequences of what you did.\r
\r
Resilient to the end. I'll survive.\r
\r
---\r
\r
And today I wanted to share this with you.\r
\r
You can see it. I work at Panorama.id. Then I work here on iFood until midnight, every single day. Sometimes into the early morning.\r
\r
And still, every second I can, thanks to artificial intelligence, I work on the project in remote-control mode, from my phone. While the phone is working on an iFood run, it's working on my project. I keep building, testing, I make a delivery, test a bit more, make another one.\r
\r
That's how everything you're getting comes out. WEB SHARD, the music, the stories, the games. LDI Gangues, Top Trumps. The games of my life, the ones I always wanted to make and never had the chance to.\r
\r
All with my art direction. Direction is all I can do, because I can't draw. At the end of the first WEB SHARD chapter there are about 15 pages in a fanzine style where I explain why I use AI for this creation.\r
\r
I stopped making excuses to myself. And I started doing it. We've been in production since June, and now we're about to launch.\r
\r
---\r
\r
This isn't a display of suffering, pain and blah blah blah.\r
\r
It's just to make a point I always stand by.\r
\r
You'll always have great excuses. The best in the world. All of them make sense, all of them can be totally valid, for not doing something.\r
\r
But when you want something done, there's only one way it happens.\r
\r
You do it.\r
\r
So, no matter the hardship, no matter this rainy day I'm riding through right now, we're going to keep doing it. I'm going to keep making things.\r
\r
This first year is guaranteed for you, and a big part of it is already done. The first season will be complete, perfect and incredible. I guarantee it.\r
\r
I really hope you like it.\r
\r
And if you don't, fuck it. I'm making my version. My view of the world. My view of everything I wanted to make.\r
\r
And yes, I make a lot of things. I do everything. And I like it. I love it. And I'm going to keep doing it.\r
\r
Fuck your criticism.\r
\r
---\r
\r
**In the fiction, it became**\r
\r
Jack's bike, the Warrior, that carries him through his hustles in *Because Jack Chose So*, is my Warrior.\r
\r
The crowd fight I took on alone became the Crowd Brawl in LDI Gangues.\r
\r
And Lester's slot car track keeps going around.\r
`;export{e as default};
